# ============================================================================
# Environment variables this app reads. Copy to `.env.local` for local work;
# set the same names in Vercel → Settings → Environment Variables for deploys.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, AND NOTHING HERE MAY BECOME `NEXT_PUBLIC_`.
# That prefix inlines a value into the JavaScript shipped to the browser, and
# every value below is a secret: the address of an internal service, the token
# that opens it, and a billable API key. They are read only in server-side code
# (`lib/operator-what-changed-api.ts` and `lib/what-changed-ai.ts`, both
# `server-only`, reached through the route handler at
# `app/api/operators/[number]/what-changed/`), so the browser sees its own
# origin and an operator number and nothing else.
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored. Do not commit a filled-in copy of this file.
# ============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The "What changed" analysis service — the Python service that reads MongoDB,
# measures the production and operational changes, and has Claude rephrase the
# findings. See mv-operator-what-changed/RUN-SERVICE.bat.
#
# LOCAL: the service runs on the same machine, so point straight at it. ngrok
# is NOT needed for localhost work.
#   WHAT_CHANGED_SERVICE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8733
#
# VERCEL: Vercel cannot reach your LAN, so this is the public https URL of the
# tunnel in front of the service. No trailing slash.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT_CHANGED_SERVICE_URL=https://your-tunnel-subdomain.ngrok-free.app

# The shared secret. Must match MV_SITE_TOKEN on the Python service exactly, or
# every request comes back 401 and the section shows "could not be reached".
# Any long random string; generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(24).toString('hex'))"
WHAT_CHANGED_SERVICE_TOKEN=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHO PHRASES THE FINDINGS.
#
# The panel is two jobs. THE MEASURED FINDINGS come from the Python analysis
# service where one is configured and reachable; where it is not — a Vercel
# deployment, or a local checkout with the service stopped — they are measured
# instead from `/api/v1/operators/details`, the same public endpoint the operator
# detail page already reads. So `WHAT_CHANGED_SERVICE_URL` above is an upgrade,
# not a requirement: it sees changes across five MongoDB collections that the
# details endpoint does not carry. THE WORDING is one HTTPS call to a model and
# needs no database, so it always runs here.
#
# ONE VARIABLE IS THE WHOLE CONFIGURATION. Set `AI_SUMMARY_KEY` and Claude
# writes the wording. `AI_PROVIDER` is only needed to choose a different vendor
# (`gemini`) or to switch the step off (`none`).
#
# Either way the wording is only ever a rewrite: the numbers, directions and
# sources are measured before a model is asked anything, and a rewrite quoting
# a figure that was not in its input is rejected and the measured sentence kept.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# An Anthropic API key, unless AI_PROVIDER names another vendor.
#
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY. Read inside a function in `lib/what-changed-ai.ts`, never
# inlined into a bundle, never returned by an API route, never rendered into
# HTML. The browser gets the finished sentences and nothing else.
#
# An unset or wrong key is not an outage: the panel falls back to the measured
# wording and says so in the badge.
AI_SUMMARY_KEY=PASTE_KEY_HERE

# Optional. `claude`, `gemini`, or `none` to leave the wording to the analysis
# service. `AI_SUMMARY_PROVIDER` — the name the map pages' summary already uses —
# is read too, so one deployment needs only one of them.
#
# UNSET IS INFERRED FROM THE KEY: one beginning `sk-ant-` is Anthropic's, anything
# else is treated as Gemini, which is what the map's summary defaults to. Set this
# only to override that.
# AI_PROVIDER=

# Optional. Defaults to claude-opus-5.
# AI_SUMMARY_MODEL=

# Optional. Seconds a piece of wording may be reused; 0 (the default) asks the
# model again on every render, so the section reads differently after a refresh.
# That is one model call per view of the section — set 3600 to go back to one
# call per hour per operator. The measured figures are unaffected either way.
# AI_SUMMARY_CACHE_SECONDS=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SERVICE HAS ITS OWN KEY, SEPARATELY.
#
# `AI_SUMMARY_KEY` above is this app's. The Python service can also phrase its
# own rows, using ANTHROPIC_API_KEY on the machine that runs it — set that in
# RUN-SERVICE.bat, not here. The two do not conflict: if the service already
# had a model write the rows, this app leaves them alone.
#
# Where a deployment has both, prefer this app's: it runs wherever the site
# runs, so it does not depend on that machine being up.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE OWNER PORTAL'S ALERTS AND ACTIVITY — `mineralview-api`.
#
# ONE VARIABLE IS THE WHOLE SWITCH, AND IT IS OFF BY DEFAULT.
#
#   unset  — `/mineralownersite/alerts` and `.../activities` render from the
#            committed capture in `app/mineralownersite/_lib/reference/
#            owner-payload.json`, exactly as they did before the service
#            existed. No request is made, so nothing can fail.
#   set    — four blocks of the owner record come from the service instead:
#              GET /api/v1/alerts            the findings and the watch ledger
#              GET /api/v1/activity          the timeline
#              GET /api/v1/activity/summary  the KPI cards and the charts
#              GET /api/v1/activity/rings    the 1 / 3 / 5-mile panel
#            Everything else on the record — the portfolio, the leases, the
#            weekly report — has no endpoint yet and stays the capture.
#
# NO TRAILING SLASH, and no `/api/v1` — the client appends that itself.
#   MINERALVIEW_API_BASE_URL=https://mview-dev-api.mineralview.com
#
# NOT `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, AND IT MUST NOT BECOME IT. It is read in `server-only`
# code (`_lib/reference/owner-api.ts`), which is what keeps the browser out of
# the conversation entirely: no address in the bundle, and no browser origin to
# add to the service's CORS allowlist.
#
# A COLD OWNER IS 23 TO 28 SECONDS — the appraisal roll carries no index on the
# owner name — and every read inside the service's 15-minute window is under a
# second. The client allows 60s per call and the portal shows its named loader
# meanwhile. Do not lower that timeout; a 10-second default aborts a request
# that was going to succeed.
#
# WHEN THE SERVICE IS UNREACHABLE the portal says so rather than quietly
# serving the capture, because a live page above last month's alerts, with
# nothing on screen admitting it, is the worse failure. Leave this unset if you
# want the capture.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MINERALVIEW_API_BASE_URL=
